<div class="legal-note"><p style="margin:0">This is a courtesy translation. The contract is governed by the <a href="{{ \App\Support\LocaleUrl::switchTo('it') }}">Italian text</a>, which prevails in case of any difference.</p></div>

<p>These terms govern the hosting, virtual server, domain name, SSL certificate and certified email (PEC) services that Esalogica sells through my.esalogica.com.</p>

<h2>Art. 1 — Provider and definitions</h2>
<p>1.1 The services are provided by <strong>{{ $esaCompany['legal_name'] }}</strong>, a sole proprietorship, registered office {{ $esaCompany['address'] }}, Italy, VAT no. IT{{ $esaCompany['vat'] }}, REA {{ $esaCompany['rea'] }}, certified email (PEC) <a href="mailto:{{ $esaCompany['pec'] }}">{{ $esaCompany['pec'] }}</a>, email <a href="mailto:{{ $esaCompany['email'] }}">{{ $esaCompany['email'] }}</a> (the "Provider").</p>
<p>1.2 In these terms:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Customer</strong>: whoever orders one or more Services, whether an individual or a legal entity.</li>
    <li><strong>Consumer</strong>: a Customer who is an individual acting for purposes outside their trade or profession (art. 3 of Italian Legislative Decree 206/2005, the Consumer Code).</li>
    <li><strong>Services</strong>: the services listed in art. 2, as described on the product page at the time of the order.</li>
    <li><strong>Client area</strong>: the panel at my.esalogica.com where the Customer orders, pays, manages the Services and asks for support.</li>
    <li><strong>Contract</strong>: these terms, the description of the Service ordered and any third-party terms referred to in arts. 10 and 11.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Art. 2 — Scope and services</h2>
<p>2.1 The Provider offers, with the features stated for each plan:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>shared hosting</strong>: web space, mailboxes and databases on servers managed by the Provider with the Plesk control panel;</li>
    <li><strong>reseller hosting</strong>: a Plesk account from which the Customer creates and manages websites for their own clients;</li>
    <li><strong>virtual private servers (VPS)</strong>: virtual machines with the resources of the chosen plan;</li>
    <li><strong>registration, renewal and transfer of domain names</strong>;</li>
    <li><strong>SSL certificates</strong> issued by third-party certificate authorities;</li>
    <li><strong>certified email (PEC) mailboxes</strong> from an accredited provider;</li>
    <li><strong>ancillary services</strong>, such as anti-spam and anti-virus protection for email and dedicated IP addresses.</li>
</ul>
<p>2.2 The features of each Service (disk space, traffic, number of mailboxes, resources, operating system) are those published on the product page at the time of the order and shown in the order summary.</p>
<p>2.3 Hosting plans paid yearly include the registration of one domain name, and its renewal, for as long as the plan remains active and paid yearly. With hosting plans paid monthly the domain name is paid separately, at the list price of the domain.</p>

<h2>Art. 3 — Formation of the contract and activation</h2>
<p>3.1 The Contract is formed when the Customer completes the order in the Client area and accepts these terms. The Provider confirms the order by email.</p>
<p>3.2 The Service is activated once payment is received, normally within 1 working day. Registering or transferring a domain and issuing an SSL certificate also depend on the time taken by the registry or certificate authority and on the checks they require.</p>
<p>3.3 The Customer warrants that the information provided is true, complete and up to date, and keeps it so from the Client area. The Provider may refuse or suspend an order with false or incomplete information, or one that appears fraudulent.</p>
<p>3.4 The Customer is responsible for keeping the login credentials of the Client area and of the Services safe, and for every operation carried out with them.</p>

<h2>Art. 4 — Term and renewal</h2>
<p>4.1 Each Service runs for the billing cycle chosen in the order (for instance monthly or yearly), starting from its activation date.</p>
<p>4.2 At expiry the Service renews for the same period unless the Customer cancels it within the deadline in art. 4.3. The renewal is at the price of the Service in force at that time, shown in the renewal notice.</p>
<p>4.3 Renewals follow a monthly cycle. On the 15th of each month the Provider sends the Customer, at the billing email address, the renewal notice: the list of Services and domain names expiring in the second following month, with their prices. The Customer can cancel from the Client area those they no longer want, by the last day of the month in which they receive the notice. On the first day of the following month the Provider invoices the renewals not cancelled (art. 5.2). For example: notice on 15 January for what expires in March, cancellation by 31 January, invoice on 1 February.</p>
<p>4.4 A renewal does not wait for payment: the Service continues without interruption at its expiry. Domain names, SSL certificates and PEC mailboxes are renewed with the registry or provider from the invoice date, before they expire; from then on their price is due even if the Customer cancels them later. A domain name cancelled in time is not renewed and at expiry follows the registry's rules (art. 10.4).</p>

<h2>Art. 5 — Prices, invoicing and payment</h2>
<p>5.1 Prices are those of the price list published at the time of the order. On renewal the price of the Service stated in the renewal notice applies, which may differ from the current price list. If the Customer asks to move to another plan, the list price of the new plan applies from then on. Prices published on the site exclude VAT; VAT is added in the order summary and on the invoice, and Consumers are shown the total price including VAT before the order is completed.</p>
<p>5.2 A new order is paid when placed and the Service is activated once payment is received (art. 3.2). Renewals are invoiced on the first day of each month, in one invoice per Customer (art. 4.3), and are payable within 30 days of the invoice date, end of month: an invoice dated 1 February is due on 31 March. Different payment terms agreed with an individual Customer are shown on the invoice and prevail. The electronic invoice is sent through the Italian Exchange System (SDI); the Customer enters their recipient code or PEC address in the Client area.</p>
<p>5.3 Accepted payment methods: credit or debit card through Stripe, PayPal and bank transfer. Card details are handled only by Stripe or PayPal and never pass through the Provider's systems. Bank charges are borne by the Customer. In a bank transfer the Customer gives the invoice number as the payment reference; the bank details are on the invoice and in the email that comes with it. If the Customer has saved a card for automatic payment, the amount is charged on the invoice due date.</p>
<p>5.4 The Provider may change renewal prices by stating them in the renewal notice, and in any case at least 30 days before expiry. A Customer who does not accept the new price may cancel the Service within the deadline in art. 4.3, at no cost.</p>

<h2>Art. 6 — Non-payment</h2>
<p>6.1 If an invoice is not paid by its due date, the Provider sends a reminder and, 15 days after the due date, may suspend the Services it relates to. While suspended, websites, mailboxes and virtual machines cannot be reached, but the data is kept.</p>
<p>6.2 The Service is reactivated within 1 working day of receiving payment.</p>
<p>6.3 If the invoice is still unpaid 30 days after its due date, the Contract for that Service is terminated and the Provider may permanently delete the data. The Customer is responsible for keeping a copy.</p>
<p>6.4 For domain names, the registry deadlines referred to in art. 10 apply.</p>
<p>6.5 For business Customers, sums not paid when due bear, without any need for formal notice, the interest provided for by Italian Legislative Decree 231/2002, plus the flat sum of 40 euros for recovery costs.</p>

<h2>Art. 7 — Withdrawal and cancellation</h2>
<p>7.1 <strong>Consumer's right of withdrawal.</strong> A Consumer may withdraw from the Contract within 14 days of its formation without giving any reason (arts. 52 and following of the Consumer Code). An explicit statement is enough, sent by email to <a href="mailto:{{ $esaCompany['email'] }}">{{ $esaCompany['email'] }}</a> or by PEC to <a href="mailto:{{ $esaCompany['pec'] }}">{{ $esaCompany['pec'] }}</a>, also using the model form at the end of these terms.</p>
<p>7.2 The Provider refunds what it received within 14 days of the notice of withdrawal, by the same means of payment the Consumer used.</p>
<p>7.3 If the Consumer asks for the Service to start before the 14 days have passed, on withdrawal they pay for the part of the Service provided until then, in proportion to the price (art. 57(3) of the Consumer Code).</p>
<p>7.4 The right of withdrawal does not apply, where the Consumer expressly asked for it and acknowledged losing the right of withdrawal once the service has been performed (art. 59(1)(a) of the Consumer Code):</p>
<ul>
    <li>to the registration or transfer of a domain name, once carried out with the registry;</li>
    <li>to the issuing of an SSL certificate, once issued by the certificate authority.</li>
</ul>
<p>7.5 <strong>Cancellation.</strong> Any Customer may cancel a Service at any time from the Client area, effective at the end of the period already invoiced or immediately, as they choose. Outside the cases in art. 7.1, the period already invoiced and not used remains due and is not refunded.</p>
<p>7.6 When a Service ends the Customer must already have saved their data: the Provider may delete it 7 days after the Service ends.</p>

<h2>Art. 8 — Customer obligations and acceptable use</h2>
<p>8.1 The Customer is solely responsible for the content, software and data they publish, store or transmit with the Services, and for their compliance with the law and with the rights of third parties.</p>
<p>8.2 The Services may not be used to:</p>
<ul>
    <li>send unsolicited email (spam), including through unauthorised forms or lists;</li>
    <li>spread malware or phishing, or content that is unlawful, defamatory or infringes copyright and trademarks;</li>
    <li>attack or scan other systems, or attempt unauthorised access;</li>
    <li>mine cryptocurrency, or run processes that keep shared resources continuously busy;</li>
    <li>resell the Services, except under reseller plans.</li>
</ul>
<p>8.3 The Customer keeps the software they install up to date (for instance WordPress, its plugins and themes) and protects their passwords. A compromised website that harms the server or other customers may be suspended at once, as provided in art. 14.</p>
<p>8.4 A reseller Customer answers to the Provider for their end customers as for themselves, and has them accept terms that include those of this article.</p>
<p>8.5 Reports of abuse or of unlawful content hosted on the Services are sent to <a href="mailto:{{ $esaCompany['email'] }}">{{ $esaCompany['email'] }}</a>, stating the address of the content and the reason.</p>

<h2>Art. 9 — Resources and backups</h2>
<p>9.1 Each plan has the limits stated in its description. On shared hosting the Provider may restrict an account whose use of resources (CPU, memory, processes, outgoing email) degrades the service for other customers, informing the Customer and, where needed, suggesting a suitable plan.</p>
<p>9.2 The disk space of each plan covers the website, databases and email stored on the server, and is a strict limit. When usage reaches 80% of the space, the Provider warns the Customer by email. Once the limit is reached the Service accepts no new data, including incoming email, which may be bounced back to the sender. To keep using it, the Customer frees space, for instance by archiving email on their own computer, or moves to a larger plan from the Client area at the list price of the new plan (art. 5.1). The Provider is not liable for bounced messages or data not saved because the space ran out.</p>
<p>9.3 The Provider backs up the shared hosting servers daily, keeping each backup for 7 days, so as to restore the service after a failure. Restoring individual files at the Customer's request is free of charge.</p>
<p>9.4 These backups do not replace the Customer's own, who remains responsible for keeping up-to-date copies of their data. On VPS, backups are the Customer's responsibility unless a backup service is bought separately.</p>

<h2>Art. 10 — Domain names</h2>
<p>10.1 The Provider registers and manages domains through accredited registrars. The domain is held in the name of the Customer, or of the person they indicate, who is its registrant.</p>
<p>10.2 The Customer accepts the rules of the registry of the chosen extension: for <code>.it</code> domains the assignment and management rules of the .it Registry, for other extensions the rules of the relevant registry and, where applicable, ICANN policies. Registrant details must be true and up to date: false details may lead the registry to revoke the domain.</p>
<p>10.3 Whether a name is available is confirmed only once it has been registered. The Provider is not liable if a name is registered by someone else before the order is completed, nor for third-party claims on the name chosen by the Customer.</p>
<p>10.4 A domain not renewed by its expiry enters the grace and redemption periods set by the registry. During those periods recovery may carry an extra charge, shown in the price list; once they are over the domain becomes free and anyone may register it.</p>
<p>10.5 The Customer may transfer the domain to another provider: the Provider gives the transfer code (AuthInfo) within 2 working days of the request made from the Client area, subject to the locks set by the registry's rules.</p>

<h2>Art. 11 — SSL certificates and PEC</h2>
<p>11.1 <strong>SSL certificates.</strong> Certificates are issued by third-party certificate authorities, of which the Provider is a reseller, following their validation procedures (email, DNS record or file on the website). The Provider is not liable for a refusal or delay in issuing caused by those checks. During its validity the certificate may be reissued at no extra cost, within the limits set by the authority.</p>
<p>11.2 <strong>PEC.</strong> Certified email mailboxes are provided by Aruba PEC S.p.A., a provider accredited with AgID, of which the Provider is a partner. The mailbox is also governed by the provider's terms and operating manual, which the Customer accepts on activation, and by the identification of the holder the provider requires.</p>

<h2>Art. 12 — Availability, maintenance and support</h2>
<p>12.1 The Provider undertakes to keep the Services available continuously, with the professional diligence of the industry. It does not guarantee the absence of interruptions, which may be caused by failures, cyber attacks or third-party networks.</p>
<p>12.2 Scheduled maintenance that causes interruptions is announced at least 48 hours in advance and carried out, as far as possible, at low-traffic times. Urgent security work may be done without notice.</p>
<p>12.3 Technical support is requested by opening a ticket from the Client area, Monday to Friday, 9:00 to 18:00 Italian time, excluding public holidays. It covers the working of the Services, not the development or fixing of the Customer's websites and software, which may be offered separately.</p>

<h2>Art. 13 — Liability</h2>
<p>13.1 The Provider is liable for damage caused by its own breach. Except for wilful misconduct or gross negligence (art. 1229 of the Italian Civil Code), and without prejudice to the rights the law grants Consumers which cannot be limited, the Provider's liability:</p>
<ul>
    <li>excludes indirect damage, such as loss of profit, customers or opportunities;</li>
    <li>does not exceed the amount paid by the Customer for the Service concerned in the 12 months before the event.</li>
</ul>
<p>13.2 The Provider is not liable for the Customer's content, for loss of data the Customer could have avoided with their own backups, or for outages caused by force majeure, third-party networks or services, or use of the Services not in line with these terms.</p>
<p>13.3 The Customer indemnifies the Provider against third-party claims arising from the content published or from use of the Services in breach of art. 8.</p>

<h2>Art. 14 — Suspension and termination</h2>
<p>14.1 The Provider may suspend a Service at once, informing the Customer as soon as possible, if:</p>
<ul>
    <li>its use breaches art. 8 or endangers the security of the servers or of other customers;</li>
    <li>a judicial or administrative authority orders it;</li>
    <li>the Customer's details turn out to be false.</li>
</ul>
<p>14.2 The Contract is terminated by operation of law under art. 1456 of the Italian Civil Code, on written notice from the Provider, in case of serious or repeated breach of art. 8, false details, or non-payment as provided in art. 6.3.</p>
<p>14.3 The Provider may withdraw from the Contract, for instance if it discontinues a Service, with at least 60 days' notice, refunding the part already paid and not used.</p>

<h2>Art. 15 — Personal data</h2>
<p>15.1 The Provider processes the Customer's data, as controller, to perform the Contract and comply with legal obligations, as described in the <a href="{{ route('legal.show', 'privacy') }}">privacy notice</a>.</p>
<p>15.2 For the personal data the Customer stores in the Services (websites, mailboxes, databases, VPS), the controller is the Customer and the Provider is its processor under art. 28 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679, on the terms of the <a href="{{ route('legal.show', 'dpa') }}">data processing agreement</a>, which forms part of the Contract.</p>
<p>15.3 Stripe and PayPal process payment data as independent controllers, under their own privacy notices.</p>

<h2>Art. 16 — Changes to these terms</h2>
<p>16.1 The Provider may change these terms because of changes in the law, in technology or in the Services, giving notice by email at least 30 days before they take effect.</p>
<p>16.2 A Customer who does not accept the changes may withdraw before they take effect, at no cost, with a refund of the part already paid and not used. Otherwise the changes apply from the date stated.</p>
<p>16.3 These terms apply to orders placed from 17 November 2026. For Customers with Services already active on that date they apply from 1 January 2027.</p>

<h2>Art. 17 — Notices and complaints</h2>
<p>17.1 Notices to the Customer are sent to the email address given in the Client area, or by PEC if provided. Notices to the Provider go to <a href="mailto:{{ $esaCompany['email'] }}">{{ $esaCompany['email'] }}</a> or to the PEC address <a href="mailto:{{ $esaCompany['pec'] }}">{{ $esaCompany['pec'] }}</a>.</p>
<p>17.2 Complaints are made with a ticket from the Client area or by PEC. The Provider answers within 15 days.</p>
<p>17.3 Consumers may also turn to the alternative dispute resolution bodies provided for by arts. 141 and following of the Consumer Code.</p>

<h2>Art. 18 — Governing law and jurisdiction</h2>
<p>18.1 The Contract is governed by Italian law.</p>
<p>18.2 For disputes with a Consumer, the court of the place where the Consumer resides or is domiciled has jurisdiction (art. 66-bis of the Consumer Code). For any other dispute the Court of Brescia has exclusive jurisdiction.</p>

<h2>Specific approval of clauses</h2>
<p>Under arts. 1341 and 1342 of the Italian Civil Code, the Customer declares that they have read and specifically approve:</p>
<ul>
    <li>arts. 4.2, 4.3 and 4.4, automatic renewal, cancellation deadline and third-party renewals due even if cancelled after the invoice;</li>
    <li>art. 5.4, changes to renewal prices;</li>
    <li>art. 6, suspension and termination for non-payment;</li>
    <li>arts. 7.5 and 7.6, no refund for the unused period and deletion of data;</li>
    <li>arts. 9.1 and 9.2, restriction of resources and blocking of the Service when the disk space limit is reached;</li>
    <li>art. 13, limitations of liability;</li>
    <li>art. 14, suspension, termination and withdrawal by the Provider;</li>
    <li>art. 16, changes to these terms;</li>
    <li>art. 18.2, jurisdiction.</li>
</ul>
<p>Towards Consumers these clauses apply only within the limits allowed by arts. 33 and following of the Consumer Code.</p>

